{"causes":["Formation of the Himalayan mountain range around 50 million years ago led to the creation of several major river systems, including the Ganges and Brahmaputra, which supported early human settlements in the region."],"effects":["The spread of Buddhism from ancient India to East Asia during the Han Dynasty (206 BCE-220 CE) had a profound impact on Chinese culture, philosophy, and art.","The Mongol Empire's conquests under Genghis Khan (1206-1227) united vast territories under a single rule, facilitating trade and cultural exchange between East and West."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Mongol Empire","sourceName":"Genghis Khan","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"Asia","targetName":"The Asian continent","context":"The Mongols united vast territories under a single rule, facilitating trade and cultural exchange between East and West during the 13th century."},{"sourceSlug":"Han Dynasty","sourceName":"Chinese Empire","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"Silk Road","targetName":"The Silk Road network of trade routes","context":"The Han Dynasty facilitated the exchange of goods, ideas, and technologies between China and the Mediterranean region during the 2nd century BCE."},{"sourceSlug":"Indus Valley Civilization","sourceName":"Saraswati River valley civilization","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Buddhism","targetName":"The Buddhist religion","context":"The Indus Valley Civilization is believed to have originated Buddhism, which later spread to China, Korea, and Japan."}],"places":[{"name":"Himalayas","role":"Geographical feature"},{"name":"Silk Road","role":"Trade route"},{"name":"Korean Peninsula","role":"Region of conflict"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Asia","type":"Reference article","slug":"asia"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q48","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asia"],"tags":["continent","Continents","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Asia is remembered as a landmark place of the Classical era, referenced in 358 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Served as a center of human activity and development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Asia is the world's largest and most populous continent — 44.6 million km² (30% of Earth's total land area) and home to 4.7 billion people (60% of the global population). It spans from the Bosphorus and the Ural Mountains in the west to the Pacific coast in the east, from the Arctic tundra in the north to the tropical islands of Southeast Asia in the south. Asia contains the world's highest mountains (the Himalayas, with Everest at 8,849 m), the world's deepest lake (Baikal, 1,642 m), the largest inland sea (Caspian Sea), and the two most populous nations in human history (China and India, each with over 1 billion people). All three major Abrahamic religions — Judaism, Christianity, and Islam — and all major Eastern religions — Hinduism, Buddhism, Confucianism, Daoism, Sikhism — originated on the Asian continent.

Asia was the cradle of the world's earliest civilisations: Mesopotamia (Sumer, c. 3500 BCE), the Indus Valley civilisation (c. 2600 BCE), the Yellow River civilisation in China (c. 2000 BCE), and the cradle of all major world religions. The Silk Road — the overland and maritime trade network connecting China to the Mediterranean — was Asia's most consequential contribution to global history: for 1,500 years (roughly 200 BCE to 1450 CE) it transmitted silk, spices, paper, gunpowder, the compass, and the bubonic plague between the civilisations of East Asia, Central Asia, South Asia, the Middle East, and Europe. The Mongol Empire (1206–1368), which originated in Central Asia and conquered territory from Korea to Hungary, was the largest contiguous land empire in history and the most comprehensive engine of Eurasian connectivity before the age of European colonialism.

The 19th and 20th centuries saw Asia's transformation from the primary object of European colonial extraction to the primary engine of global economic growth. The Japanese Meiji Restoration (1868), the Chinese Communist Revolution (1949), and the 'Four Asian Tigers' (South Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Singapore) industrial takeoffs of the 1960s–1980s demonstrated different pathways of industrialisation that cumulatively shifted the global economic centre of gravity back toward Asia. China's GDP surpassed Japan's in 2010 and is projected to surpass the United States' by the 2030s, marking what some scholars call 'the Asian century'.","subjects":["Geography","World History","Civilisation","Global History","Religion","Economics","Silk Road","East Asia"],"frameworks":["WORLD_SYSTEMS","LONGUE_DUREE","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","COMPARATIVE_HISTORY"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Asia has played a critical role in shaping global politics, economies, and cultures, from its early civilizations to its modern-day economic powerhouses.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
